{'review': '{\n  "review": {\n    "overall_status": "REJECTED",\n    "quality_score": 2.0,\n    "summary": {\n      "critical": 1,\n      "high": 1,\n      "medium": 2,\n      "low": 2,\n      "passed_checks": [\n        "No hardcoded secrets in the migration SQL",\n        "Proper use of UUID for primary keys",\n        "Proper use of TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE and default NOW()",\n        "Proper foreign key constraints with CASCADE",\n        "Unique indexes on GitHub IDs (users, repositories, pull_requests, issues) to prevent duplicates"\n      ]\n    },\n    "findings": [\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-001",\n        "severity": "critical",\n        "category": "security",\n        "title": "Plaintext API key storage",\n        "file": "migrations/001_create_users.sql",\n        "line_range": [109, 124],\n        "description": "The api_keys table stores the API key in plaintext in the \'key\' column. If the database is compromised, attackers can steal the API keys and impersonate users.",\n        "impact": "High risk of account compromise if the database is breached. API keys should be treated as secrets and hashed before storage.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Store a cryptographic hash (e.g., bcrypt, scrypt, or PBKDF2) of the API key instead of the plaintext key. Additionally, consider adding a salt and using a work factor appropriate for key derivation.",\n        "code_before": "    \\"key\\" VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,",\n        "code_after": "    \\"key_hash\\" VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,  -- Store hash of the key\\n    -- Optionally, store key prefix for user reference (first and last 4 chars) but never the full key",\n        "references": ["CWE-256: Plaintext Storage of a Password", "CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials"]\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-002",\n        "severity": "high",\n        "category": "performance",\n        "title": "Non-unique commit SHA across repositories",\n        "file": "migrations/001_create_users.sql",\n        "line_range": [40, 62],\n        "description": "The unique index on the \'sha\' column in the commits table is too restrictive because the same commit SHA can appear in multiple repositories (e.g., in forks). This will cause insert failures when attempting to store the same SHA from a different repository.",\n        "impact": "Application errors when trying to insert commits from forked repositories that share commit SHAs with the original repository. This breaks the ability to store commit history accurately.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Change the unique index to be on the combination of (repo_id, sha) to ensure uniqueness within a repository while allowing the same SHA in different repositories.",\n        "code_before": "CREATE UNIQUE INDEX \\"commits_sha_key\\" ON \\"commits\\"(\\"sha\\");",\n        "code_after": "CREATE UNIQUE INDEX \\"commits_repo_id_sha_key\\" ON \\"commits\\"(\\"repo_id\\", \\"sha\\");",\n        "references": []\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-003",\n        "severity": "medium",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Missing check constraint for non-negative counts in repositories table",\n        "file": "migrations/001_create_users.sql",\n        "line_range": [15, 32],\n        "description": "The columns stars_count, forks_count, and open_issues_count in the repositories table are defined as INTEGER with a default of 0, but there is no constraint to prevent negative values. While the application should not set negative values, a database constraint provides an additional layer of data integrity.",\n        "impact": "Risk of invalid data (negative counts) if there is a bug in the application code, leading to incorrect analytics or display issues.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Add CHECK constraints to ensure the count columns are non-negative.\\nExample: stars_count INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (stars_count >= 0)",\n        "code_before": "    \\"stars_count\\" INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\\n    \\"forks_count\\" INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\\n    \\"open_issues_count\\" INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,",\n        "code_after": "    \\"stars_count\\" INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (stars_count >= 0),\\n    \\"forks_count\\" INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (forks_count >= 0),\\n    \\"open_issues_count\\" INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (open_issues_count >= 0),",\n        "references": []\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-004",\n        "severity": "medium",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Nullable addition/deletion/total_changes columns in commits table",\n        "file": "migrations/001_create_users.sql",\n        "line_range": [40, 62],\n        "description": "The additions, deletions, and total_changes columns in the commits table are defined as nullable INTEGER. In the GitHub API, these fields are present for commits (except possibly for some edge cases). Making them NOT NULL with a default of 0 would prevent NULL values and simplify queries that aggregate these values.",\n        "impact": "Increased complexity in queries (need to handle NULLs) and potential for inconsistent data if the application fails to set these values.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Change the columns to NOT NULL with a default of 0.\\nExample: additions INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0",\n        "code_before": "    \\"additions\\" INTEGER,\\n    \\"deletions\\" INTEGER,\\n    \\"total_changes\\" INTEGER,",\n        "code_after": "    \\"additions\\" INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\\n    \\"deletions\\" INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\\n    \\"total_changes\\" INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,",\n        "references": []\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-005",\n        "severity": "low",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Missing check constraint for state in pull_requests table",\n        "file": "migrations/001_create_users.sql",\n        "line_range": [65, 85],\n        "description": "The state column in the pull_requests table is defined as VARCHAR(50) without a constraint to limit the values to valid states (e.g., \'open\', \'closed\', \'merged\'). This relies solely on the application to enforce valid states, which could lead to invalid data if the application has a bug.",\n        "impact": "Risk of invalid state values (e.g., \'unknown\', \'pending\') appearing in the database, which could cause issues in application logic that expects only certain states.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Add a CHECK constraint to limit the state to valid values.\\nExample: state VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL CHECK (state IN (\'open\', \'closed\', \'merged\'))",\n        "code_before": "    \\"state\\" VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,",\n        "code_after": "    \\"state\\" VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL CHECK (state IN (\'open\', \'closed\', \'merged\')),",\n        "references": []\n      },\n      {\n        "finding_id": "F-006",\n        "severity": "low",\n        "category": "quality",\n        "title": "Missing check constraint for state in issues table",\n        "file": "migrations/001_create_users.sql",\n        "line_range": [88, 107],\n        "description": "The state column in the issues table is defined as VARCHAR(50) without a constraint to limit the values to valid states (e.g., \'open\', \'closed\'). This relies solely on the application to enforce valid states, which could lead to invalid data if the application has a bug.",\n        "impact": "Risk of invalid state values appearing in the database, which could cause issues in application logic that expects only certain states.",\n        "suggested_fix": "Add a CHECK constraint to limit the state to valid values.\\nExample: state VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL CHECK (state IN (\'open\', \'closed\'))",\n        "code_before": "    \\"state\\" VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,",\n        "code_after": "    \\"state\\" VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL CHECK (state IN (\'open\', \'closed\')),",\n        "references": []\n      }\n    ],\n    "positive_observations": [\n      "No hardcoded secrets in the migration SQL",\n      "Proper use of UUID for primary keys",\n      "Proper use of TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE and default NOW()",\n      "Proper foreign key constraints with CASCADE",\n      "Unique indexes on GitHub IDs (users, repositories, pull_requests, issues) to prevent duplicates"\n    ]\n  }\n}'}